   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a6856353",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## High AI Exposure by Education and Age\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, we use Statistics Canada’s experimental estimates of AI exposure to see **which workers are most exposed to AI-related job transformation**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our guiding research question is:\n",
    "\n",
    "How is AI reshaping the Canadian job market?\n",
    "\n",
    "To explore this, we draw on Appendix Tables A.1 (2016) and A.2 (2021) from *Experimental Estimates of Potential Artificial Intelligence Occupational Exposure in Canada*. These tables report, for employees aged 18–64:\n",
    "\n",
    "- An **AI Occupational Exposure Index (AIOE)** for each group.\n",
    "- A **potential complementarity** score, indicating whether AI is more likely to *complement* rather than replace their work.\n",
    "- The share of workers in three categories:\n",
    "  - **High exposure, low complementarity**\n",
    "  - **High exposure, high complementarity**\n",
    "  - **Low exposure**\n",
    "\n",
    "Using the CSV versions of these tables, we:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Compute total AI exposure** for each group as  \n",
    "   `High_exposure_total_pct = High_exposure_low_comp_pct + High_exposure_high_comp_pct`.\n",
    "2. **Plot bar charts by education level** (high school or less, trades, college, bachelor’s, graduate) for 2016 and 2021.\n",
    "3. **Plot bar charts by age group** (18–24, 25–34, 35–44, 45–54, 55–64) for 2016 and 2021.\n",
    "4. **Show change over time (2016–2021)** for each education and age group.\n",
    "\n",
    "These figures let us see how AI exposure is distributed across education and age, and how that distribution has shifted between 2016 and 2021, providing insight into which segments of the Canadian workforce are most directly affected by AI.\n"
   ]
  },
